     "derivation": "The box is deliberately empty in the book — a pass-through for the Arabic form that carries the word from Akkadian to Persian. The word meant is Arabic لاذَن lāḏan (also لادن ladan, لَدَان ladān, لَذَان laḏān) 'ladanum, the gum of the rockrose'; Klein cites it in exactly this position ('cp. Akkad. ladunu, Arab. ladan, Heb. lot'). That an Arabian word for the resin existed very early is beyond doubt: Herodotus 3.112 already describes how the Arabs gather λήδανον and gives λάδανον as their own form of the name (Fraenkel, Aram. Fremdwörter p. ~174, discusses this passage), and AHD points to Old South Arabian ldn. What is not settled is the direction of the arrow: Wiktionary's Arabic entry derives لاذن from Ancient Greek λάδανον (so Greek → Arabic), while Klein/AHD have the Semitic word feed Greek. Nourai's Akkadian → Arabic is therefore one defensible reading of a genuinely disputed link, not an established fact.",
