     "derivation": "NP لادن lādan is real and is described at length by Borhan-e Qate' (p. 1876): 'نوعی از مشمومات است ... مانند دوشاب سیاه ... و آن را عنبر عسلی گویند' — the dark, honey-like fragrant gum collected from the beards of goats that browse the rockrose; Manuchehri's line quoted in the chart ('نخیزد از میان لاد لادن') is genuine. The route Arabic → Persian is Klein's explicit statement, 'Pers. ladan is an Arabic loan word', and Biruni in the Ṣaydana (quoted by Dehkhoda s.v. لادن) says of it 'عربی است و به فارسی او را لادنه گویند'. Against that, Lokotsch (no. 1286) writes 'Ar. lādan ... [aus pers. lādān ‘Strauch’]' and Asbaghi (PLA p. 240) files lādan among Persian words in Arabic with the route 'pers. > griech. > lat.' — so the borrowing direction is genuinely contested and the node is plausible, not confirmed. The gloss is the weak point: لادن is the ladanum gum, not the lotus; Mo'in's second footnote on the same page notes that today گل لادن is the nasturtium (Tropaeolum majus, Capucine). Nourai's 'lotus' looks back-formed from the Greek lōtos he hangs beneath it.",
